Loop over the types and assert the inferred return type, so the test fails when one of them regresses:

```julia
using Test
axpy_sum(a::T, x::AbstractVector{T}) where {T<:AbstractFloat} = sum(a .* x)
for T in (Float32, Float64)
    @test Base.return_types(axpy_sum, (T, Vector{T})) == [T]
    @test (@inferred axpy_sum(T(2), T[1, 2, 3])) == T(12)
end
```

`@inferred` verifies the call you make, and `Base.return_types` the signature you declare. Write the signatures for every concrete combination you ship, including `OffsetArray` or `view` inputs if the method accepts them, since stability can differ by wrapper type.

Write them as tests that do not depend on a macro's compatibility:

```julia
using Test
f(x::Int) = x + 1
g(x::Int) = x > 0 ? x : 0.0
@testset "inferred returns" begin
    @test Base.return_types(f, (Int,)) == [Int]
    @test_broken Base.return_types(g, (Int,)) == [Int]
    @test isconcretetype(only(Base.return_types(f, (Int,))))
end
```

`Base.return_types` is part of the compiler interface that other tools use, and a failing assertion is a plain test failure. Add a deliberately unstable function as a negative control for your helper (`@test !isconcretetype(only(Base.return_types(g, (Int,))))`) so you know the helper can fail.
